WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.417 --> 00:00:02.711
We're not just building condos offices.

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:05.171
We're building beauty and culture.

00:00:05.505 --> 00:00:07.716
We're building community.

00:00:07.716 --> 00:00:10.176
But between you and me, we just made over

00:00:10.176 --> 00:00:14.222
$20 million on our last project.

00:00:14.806 --> 00:00:17.517
How's that for beauty?

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.646
As I walk past Homer and Richards
screen, pass

00:00:21.646 --> 00:00:24.566
another new construction
within three blocks.

00:00:24.941 --> 00:00:27.444
I couldn't help but wonder why do this?

00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:31.489
Keep building new buildings
when we have so many old buildings

00:00:31.489 --> 00:00:34.617
that are falling apart?

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.746
You know, the building
industry accounts for

00:00:40.665 --> 00:00:44.502
40% of the entire world's
carbon footprint.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:48.256
So if we really wanted to slow down
global warming,

00:00:48.548 --> 00:00:51.676
we should be focusing
on renovating our existing

00:00:51.676 --> 00:00:54.095
building stock.

00:00:56.264 --> 00:00:58.933
We lost so much money
on our last renovation.

00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:02.228
That idiot architect missed

00:01:02.228 --> 00:01:04.314
a bunch of stuff
and the existing building,

00:01:05.315 --> 00:01:10.320
aren't we charge for every penny, but
not one of those to be a six months job.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.238
Not a whole friggin year.

00:01:12.238 --> 00:01:14.824
We just want to get in
and get it for a living.

00:01:14.824 --> 00:01:16.534
But a boom with a new building.

00:01:16.534 --> 00:01:18.453
At least it's straightforward

00:01:20.205 --> 00:01:24.918
and I think

00:01:26.294 --> 00:01:28.838
as an architect,
I'm used to hearing this all the time.

00:01:28.963 --> 00:01:32.133
But is this really the right way forward?

00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:36.721
This building came very close

00:01:36.721 --> 00:01:39.974
to being demolished
and replaced by a brand new building.

00:01:40.391 --> 00:01:46.147
But instead, it received a $200 million
renovation against all the odds.

00:01:46.356 --> 00:01:50.026
In case you haven't heard of this
building, this is the infamous Portland

00:01:50.026 --> 00:01:50.860
building.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:53.571
A 15 story municipal office building

00:01:53.571 --> 00:01:57.408
that people hated
pretty much from its inception.

00:01:57.575 --> 00:02:04.374
Not only did it look well, kind of weird,
but there were structural problems.

00:02:04.374 --> 00:02:08.211
It was built with cheap materials,
and these

00:02:08.378 --> 00:02:12.048
tiny windows
made the spaces very dark from the inside.

00:02:12.257 --> 00:02:16.302
This building actually became almost a
joke to the civil servants who work there.

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:19.597
Despite its shortcomings,
when it actually came down

00:02:19.597 --> 00:02:22.600
to deciding whether they should
demolish it or save it.

00:02:23.059 --> 00:02:27.564
Certain things came to fruition
that changed the fate of this building.

00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:33.403
The building was built in 1982
by the American architect Michael Graves,

00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:37.782
and it's considered one of the earliest
examples of postmodern architecture.

00:02:37.866 --> 00:02:41.953
Postmodernism began
in the mid-20th century as a reaction

00:02:41.953 --> 00:02:45.290
to modernism,
which people were really starting

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:48.084
to think was boring, very boring.

00:02:48.543 --> 00:02:53.047
So architects like Robert Venturi
and Michael Graves started to play around

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:57.343
with historical references,
bright colors, unconventional forms.

00:02:57.677 --> 00:03:03.099
And unlike modernism, it tried to better
connect with its context.

00:03:03.308 --> 00:03:06.853
So when they started discussing
the demolition of the Portland building,

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:09.856
an architect nominated
this building to be added

00:03:09.856 --> 00:03:12.817
to the National Register
of Historic Places.

00:03:12.984 --> 00:03:17.238
So what saved this building in the end
was its consideration

00:03:17.238 --> 00:03:20.366
as a historically
significant piece of architecture.

00:03:20.533 --> 00:03:22.952
But not every building is so lucky.

00:03:23.203 --> 00:03:28.666
These are all architecturally
significant buildings that were torn down.

00:03:28.833 --> 00:03:32.295
And that's because what's considered
heritage can be subjective

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:36.758
and it can often come down to my opinion
versus your opinion.

00:03:37.091 --> 00:03:38.635
Just look at some of this language.

00:03:38.635 --> 00:03:41.387
Historically significant architecture
must embody

00:03:41.387 --> 00:03:44.974
the distinctive characteristics of a type
period or method of construction,

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:48.853
or that represents the work of a master
or that possesses

00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:52.815
high artistic values,
or that represent a significant

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:56.945
distinguishable entity whose components
may like individual distinction.

00:03:57.028 --> 00:03:59.864
Properties must also retain integrity.

00:04:00.156 --> 00:04:02.325
And yeah, feeling is one of them.

00:04:02.533 --> 00:04:05.620
For older buildings
that don't meet any of this criteria,

00:04:05.912 --> 00:04:11.000
as soon as they reach about 50 years old
or even sooner in some buildings,

00:04:11.334 --> 00:04:15.713
they are subject to an assessment
where they look at the performance,

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:18.967
the mechanical systems,
the envelope, the structure

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.429
and we try to see how much money and time
it would take

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:27.684
to upgrade the building systems
to a building code minimum.

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:32.230
And based on that, we try to assess
the feasibility of the upgrade.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:34.357
If it's a public building.

00:04:34.357 --> 00:04:36.985
We will also try
to get the community's input

00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:40.071
and sometimes it can definitely get
a little bit political.

00:04:40.238 --> 00:04:41.739
I mean, the community's going to be

00:04:41.739 --> 00:04:46.035
the ones paying for the project,
so they need to determine

00:04:46.577 --> 00:04:50.790
are they willing to pay extra taxes
for this brand new community center

00:04:50.873 --> 00:04:55.336
or are they okay with a piecemeal
renovation of their existing one?

00:04:55.420 --> 00:04:59.465
But is this criteria
really enough to determine

00:04:59.465 --> 00:05:03.720
whether a building
should be saved or demolished?

00:05:08.266 --> 00:05:10.143
Can you show me what would happen

00:05:10.143 --> 00:05:13.813
if we were to demolish everything
and replace it with a whole new building?

00:05:14.230 --> 00:05:17.317
Let's let's say, of the same size
in concrete?

00:05:18.192 --> 00:05:18.818
Absolutely.

00:05:18.818 --> 00:05:21.487
So I'm going to go back
to the current page here.

00:05:21.779 --> 00:05:25.325
Switch from renovation
to new construction and chain destruction

00:05:25.325 --> 00:05:27.368
system from steel to concrete.

00:05:27.493 --> 00:05:34.000
And once we make that change,

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:37.211
what the project teams did is
go to renovation.

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:39.839
They were preserving
100% of this substructure.

00:05:40.256 --> 00:05:43.259
They were preserving
about 75% of their superstructure.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:48.014
And they were redoing the enclosure
100% from scratch.

00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:50.933
So let's say
we keep the preserve number to zero.

00:05:51.225 --> 00:05:51.893
And the same thing.

00:05:51.893 --> 00:05:54.479
For two years they were redoing
the interiors from scratch again.

00:05:54.687 --> 00:05:55.355
If I click on

00:05:55.355 --> 00:05:59.108
Save here, the actual achievable embodied
carbon number for the project came down

00:05:59.108 --> 00:06:03.821
from about a million cases
a year to E to about 454,000 CO2.

00:06:04.113 --> 00:06:09.619
So they got an almost 50% reduction
in embodied carbon

00:06:09.619 --> 00:06:11.454
just by doing a renovation.

00:06:11.454 --> 00:06:14.207
Now you guys might be wondering
why the heck is

00:06:14.207 --> 00:06:16.334
she talking about embodied carbon?

00:06:16.334 --> 00:06:20.797
The building industry accounts
for 40% of the world's CO2 emissions,

00:06:21.255 --> 00:06:24.133
and the earth, as you know, is burning

00:06:24.342 --> 00:06:27.678
Ambati carbon is the silent killer
in carbon emissions.

00:06:27.970 --> 00:06:31.933
It makes up almost half
of the total construction emissions.

00:06:32.058 --> 00:06:35.478
If you don't know what it is,
embodied carbon is the carbon

00:06:35.478 --> 00:06:39.482
that's created before
and after a building's life cycle.

00:06:39.607 --> 00:06:43.194
So that's where your materials
are coming from during the extraction,

00:06:43.194 --> 00:06:48.366
the manufacture, the transportation,
construction, all of them of a building.

00:06:48.491 --> 00:06:51.369
And once the buildings
reach the end of its life

00:06:51.702 --> 00:06:56.499
during the replacement and the demolition
and the deconstruction of a building

00:06:56.707 --> 00:07:00.044
and 80% of embodied
carbon can actually be reduced

00:07:00.044 --> 00:07:02.422
if it's evaluated early
in the design phase,

00:07:02.672 --> 00:07:05.675
because that's when you're determining
some of the most important factors,

00:07:05.967 --> 00:07:08.928
like the massing,
the structural framing, the support

00:07:08.928 --> 00:07:11.806
structure, the floor
and the foundation and the roof.

00:07:12.014 --> 00:07:16.477
But the problem right now is that we
almost never have enough information

00:07:16.477 --> 00:07:18.896
when all of the key decisions
are being made,

00:07:19.147 --> 00:07:22.442
for instance, whether we should renovate
or demolish a building.

00:07:22.775 --> 00:07:26.487
And this is because for us to do
an embodied carbon calculation,

00:07:26.821 --> 00:07:29.323
first of all, the architect
needs to work on a massing scheme

00:07:29.532 --> 00:07:30.616
and then they need

00:07:30.616 --> 00:07:33.703
to send that to the structural engineer
who is going to create their own model,

00:07:33.703 --> 00:07:36.873
which we would use to estimate
the structural quantities,

00:07:36.914 --> 00:07:41.461
and then we can estimate the carbon
emissions from those massing schemes.

00:07:41.669 --> 00:07:44.589
In most projects,
the timelines are so tight

00:07:44.589 --> 00:07:47.633
that we don't have the capacity to do this
every time.

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:48.843
In other cases,

00:07:48.843 --> 00:07:53.014
we will get an outside consultant
to do a full lifecycle assessment.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:56.851
But that's really expensive
and it could take months and months

00:07:57.143 --> 00:08:02.440
and either way, the calculations typically
happen way too late in the design phase.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:04.692
Okay, let's
get back to the Portland building.

00:08:05.193 --> 00:08:08.571
So did you create this
just based off an architectural

00:08:08.571 --> 00:08:12.033
massing model or did you have to put in
any structural information?

00:08:12.116 --> 00:08:12.992
What we have done

00:08:12.992 --> 00:08:16.662
is basically use the architectural massing
of the actual building geometry

00:08:17.205 --> 00:08:17.997
and apply

00:08:17.997 --> 00:08:21.417
the principles of structural engineering
in the back in an automated fashion

00:08:21.792 --> 00:08:24.504
to estimate the quantities
of different structural elements.

00:08:24.795 --> 00:08:27.924
So even if you don't have
the quantity information for how much,

00:08:27.965 --> 00:08:31.052
how many columns do you have
in the building or beams or slabs?

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:36.015
We have estimated those quantities to
about an 85 to 95% accuracy automatically.

00:08:36.265 --> 00:08:38.351
So you don't need
structural engineers anymore.

00:08:38.351 --> 00:08:42.271
I think what we are trying to do is bridge
a big gap that we saw in the industry

00:08:42.271 --> 00:08:45.608
is the availability of information
when the decisions are being made.

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:49.028
So at no point do you trying to replace
any expert on the project.

00:08:49.237 --> 00:08:52.031
What we are doing is basically providing
designers of the project team

00:08:52.031 --> 00:08:55.117
members a starting point,
but then the engineers jump on the project

00:08:55.326 --> 00:08:57.286
when they have more accurate information.

00:08:57.286 --> 00:09:01.082
They can always just override manually
this information and make better decisions

00:09:01.082 --> 00:09:05.628
off that by having access to this
information early in the design process.

00:09:05.878 --> 00:09:09.257
We can have a much bigger impact
on the sustainability of a building.

00:09:09.465 --> 00:09:12.426
So they just passed
a new international building code

00:09:12.426 --> 00:09:16.514
saying that we can build a building
in heavy timber up to 18 storeys

00:09:16.514 --> 00:09:18.766
and engineered wood up to 15 storeys,

00:09:19.016 --> 00:09:21.185
which is the same size
as the Portland building.

00:09:21.519 --> 00:09:23.854
So yeah, can we see the difference

00:09:23.896 --> 00:09:27.608
if we were to build this out of timber
from the beginning?

00:09:27.942 --> 00:09:30.069
Let's switch it to new construction

00:09:30.528 --> 00:09:33.948
and switch the structural system type
from concrete to mass timber.

00:09:34.282 --> 00:09:36.826
And once we do that,
you can see how the numbers is updated.

00:09:36.993 --> 00:09:39.537
And even if you're going
into a new construction with mass timber,

00:09:39.870 --> 00:09:41.247
the total achievable embodied

00:09:41.247 --> 00:09:44.166
carbon emissions for your project
would still remain fairly low

00:09:44.375 --> 00:09:47.712
as compared to a new construction done in
concrete or maybe in steel.

00:09:49.046 --> 00:09:49.338
Yeah,

00:09:49.338 --> 00:09:53.175
because what we saw before with concrete,
that was about a million, right?

00:09:53.301 --> 00:09:54.635
Yeah, that was pretty high. Yeah.

00:09:54.635 --> 00:09:59.849
So if we had this in mind from
the beginning, we could have saved around

00:09:59.849 --> 00:10:04.604
700,000 cages. CO2.

00:10:04.604 --> 00:10:06.647
Although the decision
for saving the Portland

00:10:06.647 --> 00:10:10.151
building was not necessarily due
to its environmental impact.

00:10:10.443 --> 00:10:13.404
You can actually see
just how much of a difference

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:14.947
that made to the carbon emissions.

00:10:14.947 --> 00:10:19.201
And not just that the renovation
has significantly improved

00:10:19.201 --> 00:10:23.414
the building's building performance
and now it's on track to earn LEED Gold.

00:10:23.497 --> 00:10:26.792
The ironic part
is that now after the renovation,

00:10:27.126 --> 00:10:31.255
the building might actually lose
its status on the National Register

00:10:31.255 --> 00:10:35.468
of Historic Places because apparently
it's been changed too much.

00:10:35.635 --> 00:10:38.346
I'm sure as we get more
and more aging buildings

00:10:38.346 --> 00:10:42.016
and as we get better energy measures
in the construction industry,

00:10:42.016 --> 00:10:46.145
we will be asking ourselves
how we can preserve the integrity

00:10:46.145 --> 00:10:49.065
of the historic building
while improving its function

00:10:49.231 --> 00:10:52.360
and hopefully embody carbon becomes not

00:10:52.360 --> 00:10:56.906
a sustainable city target,
but just a part of our everyday practice.

00:10:57.031 --> 00:10:57.990
As architects.

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.703
We can start by adopting apps
like Cove Tool into our everyday toolbox.

00:11:02.745 --> 00:11:05.748
So comparing
embodied carbon is just another

00:11:05.748 --> 00:11:08.834
part of the design process,
like doing a Sun study.

00:11:08.918 --> 00:11:13.339
And we can also have these conversations
with the stakeholders really early on

00:11:13.339 --> 00:11:14.674
in the design process.

00:11:14.674 --> 00:11:18.969
But until then, tools like this
can empower us.

00:11:19.095 --> 00:11:21.764
We can have conversations
about the practicality

00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:25.726
of tearing down a building or
renovating it from an energy standpoint.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:29.772
And because we can have this data
as early as pre design,

00:11:29.855 --> 00:11:33.776
we can actually have a really big impact
on the carbon footprint of a project.

00:11:34.026 --> 00:11:35.444
So what do you guys think?

00:11:35.444 --> 00:11:38.364
What kind of changes do you think
we need to make in the building industry?

00:11:38.406 --> 00:11:39.699
Let me know in the comments.

00:11:39.699 --> 00:12:42.720
And with that,
I'll see you guys in the next video.

